---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-attentive';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-attentive');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-attentive');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Drip handle SMS as well as email?",
    "answer": "Its current SMS activation guide limits availability to US customers with Support All People enabled, subject to review and sending-number registration. Confirm account approval before relying on it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Attentive only an SMS option?",
    "answer": "No. Attentive documents triggered emails for cart and Shopify checkout abandonment, with access requested through its team."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both tools send for the same cart?",
    "answer": "Only with an explicit channel owner, shared exclusions and demonstrated purchase exits. Start with one recovery sender while validating any handoff."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the examples observed results?",
    "answer": "No. These are proposed acceptance exercises and hypothetical gross-profit calculations, not measured lift or a product ranking."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose who owns the customer’s next reminder before choosing a channel mix. This comparison focuses on an existing Drip email program considering Attentive, or a move to one combined recovery journey.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate Drip", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Keep the existing email workflow if its cart events and eligible SMS path meet the brief."}, {"label": "Evaluate Attentive", "tool": "Attentive", "reason": "Consider combined journeys after confirming email access and the migration scope."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>Attentive</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Channel scope</td><td>Email plus account-conditional SMS activation</td><td>Triggered email access must be confirmed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proof to request</td><td>Identified cart event and correct queued product block</td><td>Enabled checkout journey and exclusion behavior</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost comparison</td><td>Retained workflow labor plus approved channel usage</td><td>Offered channels, service scope and migration labor</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Decide between a handoff and a replacement</h2>
<p>If Drip already sends email, adding a second recovery sender creates an ownership decision: retain Drip email and give Attentive a distinct SMS branch, or move the recovery journey after validating both channels. Neither option establishes a winner from a feature list. Ask the operator to show which system cancels each pending message after a purchase and which system owns the next eligible touch.</p>
<h2>Confirm both offered accounts</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924885546125-How-to-Activate-SMS">Drip’s SMS activation guide</a> currently restricts availability to US customers with Support All People enabled. Review and number registration precede activation; workflow SMS actions are unavailable until then. Do not budget a launch around unapproved access. <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408727834772-Emails-in-journeys">Attentive’s email journey guide</a> lists cart and Shopify checkout abandonment and asks merchants to contact its team for access. Obtain confirmation for the actual account and recipient markets. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Use one cart to challenge the handoff</h2>
<p>Proposed exercise: begin with an identified repeat buyer, record the cart/customer key and currency, then update a variant before checkout. Drip distinguishes identified add-to-cart activity from checkout events, and warns that products added after an email is queued will not appear in it. See its <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">cart-event guide</a>. Inspect the queued content rather than assuming it always reflects the live cart. Repeat the exercise in the offered Attentive journey without assuming identical trigger semantics.</p>
<p>Next purchase while the first reminder waits. Record both systems’ exit timestamps and try an event replay. A single checkout must not create duplicate sends. Test email-only consent, SMS-only consent, an opt-out during the wait and an unrecognized visitor. A shared profile is not evidence of permission for every channel. Set a store-specific contact cap and quiet-hour policy, then demonstrate that the two queues respect it; the proposed schedule is not a universal 30-minute/4-hour ladder.</p>
<h2>Make the message solve the checkout problem</h2>
<p>For an international shipping objection, email can explain the current destination quote and delivery terms; an SMS, if eligible, should link to that answer rather than repeat the same paragraph. Route gift-message and sizing questions to support with the selected variant. Suppress unavailable items and offer a separately permitted back-in-stock route. A subscription toggle needs confirmation of cadence and terms, not automatic urgency.</p>
<p>For financing or BNPL friction, send the supported payment-help page without inventing approval or savings. Check a bundle’s existing discount before adding another code. A B2B cart below MOQ needs a quantity or sales-assistance path. For a repeat buyer, verify whether an existing benefit already resolves the objection. These are acceptance cases to run, not evidence that either product detects every objection automatically.</p>
<h2>Price the extra channel against gross profit</h2>
<p>Hypothetical $691 cart at 44% gross margin: fixed goods cost is $386.96 and full-price gross profit is $304.04. An 8% code reduces revenue to $635.72 and gross profit to $248.76, a $55.28 reduction. A separate $621 cart at 54% margin has $335.34 full-price gross profit; a 13% code leaves $254.61, costing $80.73. Neither incentive is a recommended cap. These figures exclude shipping subsidy, payment fees, returns, channel costs and labor.</p>
<p>Request quotes for the same reachable audience, monthly email volume, SMS segments, markets and support obligations. Include Drip work retained during overlap, Attentive onboarding, template rebuilding and staff time reconciling identities. Measure deduplicated orders and net contribution against a preselected holdout with adequate sample size; do not add the two dashboards’ attributed revenues or claim a fixed attribution-overstatement percentage.</p>
<h2>Cut over without two live recovery owners</h2>
<p>Export consent provenance, exclusions, templates and active-flow inventory. Rebuild the chosen cart journey first and validate checkout links, purchase exits and replies on controlled records. Assign each in-flight cart to the old queue or the new queue; pause old entry before opening new entry. Do not assume waiting contacts migrate automatically. Keep a rollback record with the last accepted event and message per cart so restarting the old flow cannot replay a reminder. Release additional browse or VIP branches only after the cart owner signs off.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
